<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">5th Dalai Lama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1617">1617</a>–<a href="/years/1682">1682</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Fifth Dalai Lama, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso, Great Fifth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 5th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso, was the first Dalai Lama to hold political power over all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. In 1642 his patron <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> of the Khoshut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> defeated the Tsangpa kings and handed him authority, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a> government. Known as the Great Fifth, he began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school and visited the Qing court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1652–1653.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama.jpg" alt="5th Dalai Lama" width="320" height="520" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thangka_of_the_5th_Dalai_Lama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death in 1653 his son Sengge succeeded him but was murdered in 1670, and Sengge's brother Galdan, who had studied as a Buddhist monk in Tibet, returned to take power, defeated his rivals and received the title Boshugtu Khan from the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b> around 1678.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b> converted some Bon monasteries to the Gelug school by force, yet officially recognised Bon as a Tibetan religion, and Samten Karmay sees his choice of a Dru son as Panchen Lama as a gesture of reconciliation.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1642 the Oirat ruler Güshi Khan conferred authority over Tibet on the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b>, whose Ganden Phodrang government ruled from Lhasa under Mongol and, from 1720, Qing protection.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1642 Güshi Khan of the Khoshut Khanate defeated the Tsangpa dynasty and conferred authority over Tibet on the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b>, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a></li><li><blockquote>Güshi Khan took the title King of Tibet but handed civil and religious authority to the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b> in the Ganden Phodrang, keeping military power and protecting the Gelug.</blockquote><a href="/state/khoshut-khanate">Khoshut Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Güshi Khan handed authority to the <b>Fifth Dalai Lama</b>, founding the Ganden Phodrang and the Khoshut Khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsangpa-dynasty">Tsangpa dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
